-## Results, history, and guarantees
-
-Apply revalidates roots, paths, identity/size/timestamps, and full content before
-changes. Copying uses exclusive destination creation and verifies contents before
-removing a moved source. Existing destination files/directories are preserved;
-conflicts never grant overwrite permission. Repeated destinations or sources
-within a plan are rejected. Filesystem/home roots, overlapping roots, symlinks,
-junctions, and reparse-point components are rejected. On Windows, actual junction
-checks were validated outside the sandbox; real file-symlink tests may skip when
-the required privilege is absent.
-
-The CLI records apply attempts in `taskmaster.db` in its working directory. The
-Python API records history only when supplied a Database or history callback.
-Preview and duplicate reports leave even existing history/index data unchanged.
-Explicit legacy indexing is possible only with `record_index=True` and an
-explicit Database; it is separate from read-only reporting. Organization no
-longer updates the duplicate index as a hidden post-file step.
-
-Each result separates `filesystem_status`, `destination_created`, `source_removed`,
-`history_status`, and `history_error` from the overall `status` and file error.
-A successful file change followed by a history failure is `completed_with_errors`,
-not a claim that the file was unchanged. A write/metadata failure can leave a new
-partial file; a failed source unlink can leave a complete destination and source.
-Results report these facts, and the batch continues. Inspect those paths before
-retrying. New partial files and newly created parent directories are not rolled
-back. Existing history databases receive additive outcome columns without losing
-old rows; historical outcomes that were never captured remain `unknown`.
-
-Success/preview uses exit 0; per-file or history failures use 1; invalid CLI
-arguments use 2. Skip is a reported nonfailure. Summary success counts include
-completed file changes with history failures; a separate history-failure count
-and exit 1 expose the persistence problem.
-
-Path/identity checks are practical safeguards, not an OS security sandbox. Another
-process can still change a path between checking and opening/unlinking it. Do not
-apply plans in actively modified or untrusted trees. Reading files may update OS
-access times; preview makes no intentional content, directory, or persistence
-writes. Move/rename use copy-plus-unlink and do not guarantee atomicity or full
-ACL/alternate-stream preservation. Filesystem and SQLite transactions are not a
-single atomic unit, and no exactly-once/crash-recovery guarantee is made.
